WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.710
Self-inspection is a
systematic internal review

2
00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:05.200
of whether pharmaceutical
operations follow

3
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.200
Good Manufacturing Practice and the
company quality system. It helps identify

4
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:15.220
weaknesses before they affect patients or
product quality. A self-inspection program

5
00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:17.850
should cover relevant people, premises,

6
00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:20.980
equipment, operations
and records. The purpose

7
00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:25.340
is to find and correct genuine
problems, not simply to produce

8
00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:26.450
a favorable score.

9
00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:30.170
A useful self-inspection
program defines scope,

10
00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:33.770
responsibilities, frequency
and follow-up. Planning

11
00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:37.330
can consider process
criticality, past deviations,

12
00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:40.350
previous findings and changes. Inspectors

13
00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:45.730
should be sufficiently knowledgeable and objective
for the activity. Risk-based prioritization

14
00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:50.910
helps allocate attention, but it does
not justify omitting required GMP

15
00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:54.870
controls. The plan should be documented
under the site quality system.

16
00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:59.970
Before an inspection, identify the
approved procedures and records relevant to

17
00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:05.230
the selected area. Examples may include
training records, equipment logs, cleaning

18
00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:08.210
records, deviations, change controls and

19
00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:11.490
qualification status.
Inspectors should check the

20
00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:16.090
current approved version and whether
records reflect actual work. A tidy

21
00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:20.610
folder alone does not demonstrate that
an operation is controlled. Evidence

22
00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.530
must be reliable and traceable.

23
00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:25.970
During the inspection,
observe work, interview

24
00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:28.150
personnel and compare actual practices

25
00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:32.790
with approved requirements. Follow the
process from material or personnel entry

26
00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:38.290
through operations and recording. Ask
neutral questions and document facts, not

27
00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:43.070
assumptions. When a possible issue is
found, capture enough objective evidence

28
00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:48.310
to allow fair assessment. Escalate any
immediate product quality or patient

29
00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:50.560
safety concern through the site procedure.

30
00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:53.890
Inspection findings should
clearly describe the

31
00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:56.680
observed condition,
applicable requirement or

32
00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:01.820
procedure, and supporting evidence. The
organization should assess the significance and

33
00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:04.830
potential product impact of each finding.

34
00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:07.580
Classification terminology
varies between quality

35
00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:12.240
systems and authorities; do not assign
a universal severity label without

36
00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.270
an applicable framework.
Reports should distinguish

37
00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:18.320
verified observations
from recommendations and

38
00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:20.210
should assign accountable follow-up.

39
00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:25.570
Appropriate corrective action should address
the causes of confirmed deficiencies, not

40
00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:30.170
just the visible symptom. Action plans
need responsible owners and justified

41
00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:34.130
timelines. Some issues
require investigation,

42
00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:36.710
product impact assessment,
preventive measures or

43
00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:43.350
training. Follow-up should verify implementation
and, where appropriate, effectiveness. Closing a

44
00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:47.150
finding solely because a document
was signed does not prove that

45
00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:49.070
the underlying problem is resolved.

46
00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:55.470
Inspection readiness is sustained through
routine GMP compliance, not a last-minute

47
00:02:55.470 --> 00:03:01.270
cleanup before an audit. Current procedures,
complete records, trained personnel and

48
00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:04.270
equipment status controls
help teams explain

49
00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:07.030
and demonstrate their
processes. Management

50
00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:12.410
review and trend analysis can reveal
recurring weaknesses. Personnel should answer

51
00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:15.790
accurately, acknowledge
uncertainty and retrieve

52
00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:19.290
evidence through approved
systems. Never fabricate

53
00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:22.150
or backdate records to
satisfy an inspector.

54
00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:28.370
Consider an internal inspection of a
sterile manufacturing support area. An

55
00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:33.030
inspector notices that an equipment
calibration label is expired while a

56
00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:37.030
nearby logbook is incomplete. The
team should secure the equipment as

57
00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:42.330
required, verify the actual calibration
and use status, and assess potential

58
00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:47.630
impact on operations or product quality.
The observation and decisions should

59
00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:52.070
be documented and escalated.
A new label alone is not an

60
00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:56.010
adequate corrective action without
confirming the underlying status.
